0250: memory growth over a session (LM issue #34): cache sizes, dead EFB copy destinations, shader modules

* aurora_mem_stats() (include/aurora/mem_stats.h): live textures (count and bytes, every
  TextureRef), texture object/content/TLUT caches, EFB copy textures and the copy-texture cache
  (with bytes), bind groups, samplers, pipelines, shader modules, EFB copy-to-RAM entries and byte
  buffers. The port logs them in its [mem] lines.
* EFB copy textures of destinations the game no longer uses. Each new copy destination (a map's
  buffers land elsewhere in the heap after a map change) kept its copy textures, one per (size,
  format, replay generation) at render resolution, for good: GPU memory grew with every map
  visited. Every 64 frames, with the FIFO drained (gx::texture::end_frame): copies whose memory the
  CPU has overwritten lose their copy textures (efb_ram::sweep; also while frame interpolation
  replays tapes, and the replay state slots lose the destination too), copy-texture cache entries
  not copied into for 1800 frames are dropped, and beyond 128 destinations the least recently
  copied go. Byte buffers no tracked copy uses are released.
* Shader modules: Dawn keeps a module's parsed shader program (Tint, ~1 MB for a GX shader) while
  anything outside Dawn references the module, and only builds it for a shader it compiles (not
  for one loaded from its cache). 0227's session-long module cache so kept every module compiled
  on a cache miss: a cold start held 2.1 GB (default settings) to 8 GB (Enhanced lighting at 120
  fps), slowed the compile down and made the exit take minutes. The cache keeps the
  latest 384 modules and is released whenever no pipeline is left to build.

